Review: Plan is pure and unit-tested on a two-CPU /proc/interrupts sample, including the management IRQs it must leave alone and wraparound with one CPU. Spread writes only interrupts that are elsewhere. Failures are logged, never returned, and it runs in its own goroutine, so it can't affect a takeover. install.sh disables irqbalance only if the unit exists (sh -n passes). Not run on ENA hardware: local VMs have no ENA interrupts, so Spread is a no-op there. go vet, go test, golangci-lint (darwin and linux) pass apart from pre-existing errcheck hits from a newer linter. Safety claim: it touches only /proc/irq affinity of interrupts named <iface>-Tx-Rx-<n>, asserted by the tests, unproven on AWS.
